Dear Mr. Hurd:



I request reconsideration of the designation of this request as a "Commerci=
al Use" request. Although =C2=A7 200.80(e)(2)(iii) identifies a requester f=
ee-category designation as an adverse determination, the August 4 letter do=
es not appear to comply with the requirements for an adverse-determination =
letter under =C2=A7 200.80(e)(2)(iv), including a brief statement of reason=
s and the required appeal notice and instructions. Please reconsider the de=
signation and supplement the fee-category record. I reserve all rights to s=
eek administrative review of any adverse determination.



Please continue to process the request and place it in the Complex Track. T=
his response confirms that WhyDRS intends to pursue the request and is not =
abandoning it.



This response addresses the commercial-use classification factor by factor.=
 Separately, it supplements the pending request for a waiver or reduction o=
f fees under =C2=A7 200.80(g)(12) with evidence concerning the public signi=
ficance of the requested records, WhyDRS' established dissemination activit=
y, and the intended editorial use of the records. The fee-waiver material i=
s a supplement to and request for action on that pending request, not an ap=
peal from a fee-waiver denial.



To avoid doubt, this fee-waiver supplement does not narrow, withdraw, or ot=
herwise modify the scope of Request No. 26-00500-FOIA. I continue to reques=
t a search for and production of all records described in the original requ=
est. The alternative request below for a partial fee waiver concerns only t=
he allocation of fees under 17 CFR =C2=A7 200.80(g)(12)(iv), not the scope =
of the search or production.





Commercial-use classification



=C2=A7=C2=A0200.80(g)(2)(i) defines a commercial-use request as one seeking=
 information "for a use or purpose that furthers" a commercial, trade, or p=
rofit interest. The rule further provides that the Office of FOIA Services =
will determine commercial-use status "on a case-by-case basis based on the =
requester's intended use of the information."



The requested records are not sought to further any commercial, trade, or p=
rofit interest of WhyDRS or of any person on whose behalf WhyDRS is acting.



The intended uses are public research, publication, public-interest advocac=
y, and increasing public understanding of the governmental and regulatory r=
esponse to the November 27=E2=80=9328, 2025 CME Group trading outage. WhyDR=
S intends to analyze the responsive material, exercise editorial judgment i=
n identifying and contextualizing information of public significance, and d=
isseminate the resulting research and analysis to the public. The records a=
re not being requested for resale, proprietary trading, commercial competit=
ive intelligence, commercial litigation, or the development or promotion of=
 a commercial product or service.



The fact that the requested records concern financial markets does not make=
 their intended use commercial. WhyDRS will not use the records to trade se=
curities or derivatives, advise paying clients, provide preferential inform=
ation to investors, or confer an exclusive informational advantage on any m=
ember, donor, sponsor, or other private party. The records and resulting an=
alysis are intended for open public dissemination on the same terms to ever=
yone.



WhyDRS also is not serving as an intermediary for a commercial requester. N=
o broker-dealer, investment adviser, fund, exchange, clearing organization,=
 issuer, law firm, litigant, data broker, or other for-profit enterprise co=
mmissioned this request or will control the use or distribution of the resp=
onsive records. The request was initiated as part of WhyDRS' own public-int=
erest research and editorial work.



Any incidental reputational benefit associated with publishing public-inter=
est research is not the use or purpose for which the records are sought and=
 does not supply the commercial, trade, or profit interest required by =C2=
=A7 200.80(g)(2)(i). Treating the ordinary visibility that can accompany pu=
blic dissemination as a commercial purpose would improperly collapse the re=
gulation's distinct news-media category into the commercial-use category, e=
ven though =C2=A7 200.80(g)(2)(vi) expressly recognizes gathering informati=
on, applying editorial judgment, and distributing a distinct work to an aud=
ience as news-media activity.



This conclusion does not depend on nonprofit status alone. It follows from =
the concrete use of the records: investigation of agency conduct, editorial=
 analysis, preservation of source materials, and unrestricted dissemination=
 of the resulting work. Advancing WhyDRS' public-education and policy missi=
on is not itself a commercial use because the mission is not carried out by=
 selling the requested information or converting access to it into revenue.



The breadth of the request, the number of potentially responsive pages, and=
 the amount of agency labor required for review also do not establish a com=
mercial purpose. Those circumstances may affect processing, but =C2=A7 200.=
80(g)(2)(i) makes the requester's intended use the basis for classification=
. The cost of responding cannot substitute for evidence that disclosure wou=
ld further an identified commercial, trade, or profit interest.



Nor will WhyDRS retain the records as a proprietary informational asset. Pu=
blic release will eliminate, rather than preserve, any exclusivity: investo=
rs, researchers, journalists, market participants, and the SEC itself will =
be able to inspect the same source material and evaluate WhyDRS' conclusion=
s independently. That use is the opposite of obtaining information for priv=
ate exploitation or competitive advantage.



Accordingly, the request does not fall within =C2=A7 200.80(g)(2)(i).



Please reclassify the request as noncommercial. At minimum, the appropriate=
 residual category is "all other requesters" under =C2=A7 200.80(g)(3)(i)(D=
), for which review fees are not chargeable.



In addition, to the extent the Office requires designation within a preferr=
ed fee category, WhyDRS requests classification as a representative of the =
news media under =C2=A7 200.80(g)(2)(vi). The requested information concern=
s a matter of potential interest to a segment of the public; WhyDRS intends=
 to gather the responsive information, use editorial judgment to transform =
those raw materials into distinct published research and analysis, and dist=
ribute that work to a public audience.



The August 4 letter states only that WhyDRS "is considered a 'Commercial Us=
e' requester." It does not identify the commercial, trade, or profit intere=
st allegedly furthered by disclosure or explain how the intended use of the=
se records satisfies =C2=A7 200.80(g)(2)(i).



Because a fee-category designation is expressly an adverse determination un=
der =C2=A7 200.80(e)(2)(iii), and =C2=A7 200.80(e)(2)(iv) requires an adver=
se-determination letter to include a brief statement of the reasons for the=
 determination, please identify:



the specific commercial, trade, or profit interest that the Office believes=
 would be furthered by disclosure;

the facts upon which the Office relied in identifying that interest;

the intended use of the records that the Office attributed to WhyDRS;

whether the Office considered WhyDRS's intended public research, publicatio=
n, advocacy, and dissemination of the information; and

the basis for concluding that the request satisfies =C2=A7 200.80(g)(2)(i);

the direct connection between disclosure and any economic benefit the Offic=
e believes WhyDRS or another person would receive; and

the existence and estimated magnitude of any commercial interest considered=
 under =C2=A7 200.80(g)(12)(iii), and how that interest was weighed against=
 the public interest in disclosure.




Absent such a commercial interest, the approximately 80 hours identified in=
 the August 4 letter as necessary to "review" the approximately 3,990 pages=
 cannot properly be assessed as =C2=A7 200.80(g)(3)(iii) review fees.





Request for waiver or reduction of fees



Independently of the requested fee-category correction, WhyDRS requests a w=
aiver of all fees under 5 USC =C2=A7 552(a)(4)(A)(iii) and 17 CFR =C2=A7 20=
0.80(g)(12).



Please treat this response as a supplement to my request for a waiver or re=
duction of fees. It addresses each consideration in =C2=A7 200.80(g)(12)(ii=
).



The subject directly concerns identifiable government operations. This requ=
est does not seek records merely to examine the conduct of CME Group as a p=
rivate company. It seeks the SEC's own contemporaneous communications, moni=
toring, interagency coordination, analysis, and response to a major market-=
infrastructure outage whose effects reached SEC-regulated securities market=
s and the price-discovery process on which those markets depend. Records sh=
owing what SEC personnel knew, what risks they evaluated, whether and how t=
hey coordinated with the CFTC or other regulators, and what action they con=
sidered or took concern identifiable activities of the Federal Government t=
hrough a direct and clear connection.



The requested records would add information that is not already public. CME=
 Group has since reported in its https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/11=
56375/000115637526000009/cme-20251231.htm =C2=A0that human error caused a c=
ritical cooling failure at its largest data center, that CME decided to hal=
t its markets, and that those markets opened late the next day. Those publi=
c facts do not reveal the SEC's contemporaneous understanding of the incide=
nt; which divisions or offices monitored it; what spillover, liquidity, pri=
ce-discovery, investor-protection, or systemic-risk concerns staff identifi=
ed; what information the SEC received from CME, the CFTC, clearing organiza=
tions, or market participants; or whether staff identified regulatory or op=
erational lessons. Disclosure therefore would not duplicate the existing pu=
blic account. It would explain the government's response to it.



Disclosure would reach and inform a reasonably broad audience. I have demon=
strated both subject-matter expertise and the ability to turn dense regulat=
ory material into a distinct public work. My https://www.sec.gov/comments/s=
r-occ-2025-801/srocc2025801-598095-1737722.pdf =C2=A0is a 72-page editorial=
 analysis of clearing, liquidity, operational concentration, cross-market r=
isk, and investor protection. It traces WhyDRS' origin to the unprecedented=
 shutdown of market operations on January 28, 2021; examines how centralize=
d operational chokepoints and outages impair investors; and synthesizes sta=
tutes, Commission materials, congressional findings, market records, and co=
mmunity research into policy recommendations. This is concrete evidence of =
past dissemination and editorial use, not a speculative assertion that resp=
onsive records might someday be posted.



I have also already begun the specific editorial project for which these re=
cords are requested. On December 3, 2025=E2=80=94the day this request was s=
ubmitted=E2=80=94I participated in an https://x.com/i/spaces/1vOxwdAEnAPKB/=
peek =C2=A0devoted entirely to the cause, effect, and aftermath of the Nove=
mber 28 CME shutdown. The discussion explained the loss of price discovery,=
 questioned the apparent failure or delayed use of redundant infrastructure=
, traced how cross-margin arrangements could transmit stress between future=
s, securities, and options clearing, and considered the danger of concentra=
ting globally important market functions in a single operational chokepoint=
. During that program I announced this FOIA request and explained that the =
government records were needed to test the public explanations and inform t=
he continuing analysis. https://dao.whydrs.org/docs/discord/meetings#taking=
-stock =C2=A0is an hour-long program for hosts, guests, contributors, and l=
ive listeners which has previously attained over 1,000 live listeners. The =
completed program therefore supplies direct evidence of an existing publica=
tion, audience, editorial process, and subject-specific intent to dissemina=
te=E2=80=94not merely a future plan conditioned on receiving free records.



I will use the responsive records to complete that existing work. WhyDRS wi=
ll preserve the non-exempt records in its publicly accessible FOIA archive =
and I will use editorial judgment to publish a source-linked follow-up expl=
aining the SEC's response, comparing the agency record with the presently a=
vailable public account, identifying cross-market and investor-protection i=
mplications, and presenting the results to investors, journalists, research=
ers, policymakers, and the general public through the same established publ=
ic-communication channels. The records will therefore be contextualized, te=
sted against other sources, and explained rather than merely placed online =
as an unexamined document dump.



The contribution to public understanding would be significant. The outage d=
id not affect an isolated or peripheral service. As https://www.reuters.com=
/business/cme-trading-halted-due-cooling-issue-data-centers-2025-11-28/ , i=
t temporarily removed ordinary price discovery and hedging across Treasury,=
 equity-index, commodity, and foreign-exchange futures. Markets in differen=
t assets do not operate as sealed compartments: futures prices inform secur=
ities trading and valuation, while margin and clearing arrangements can con=
nect obligations across futures, equities, and options. The SEC and CFTC ha=
ve themselves previously emphasized that cross-market linkages require comm=
unication and meaningful coordination of trading pauses and halts in their =
https://www.sec.gov/files/sec-cftc-prelimreport.pdf . The November outage t=
herefore raises a substantial public question about what the SEC observed a=
nd did when a failure originating in futures infrastructure impaired price =
discovery relevant to securities markets.



The comparatively thin holiday session does not make the event insignifican=
t. It makes the event a warning: a lengthy failure occurred under relativel=
y favorable volume conditions, yet one infrastructure problem still interru=
pted markets relied upon around the world. That creates an unusually concre=
te opportunity to evaluate safeguards before the same class of failure occu=
rs during heavy volume, an expiring-contract window, or broader market stre=
ss. CME's later conclusion that the incident was not material to its own bu=
siness does not answer whether the interruption exposed material risks to i=
nvestors, price formation, regulated intermediaries, or linked clearing sys=
tems.



The public explanation also does not establish how federal regulators evalu=
ated the adequacy of redundancy, contingency, and failover arrangements dur=
ing the event. The requested records could show what risks SEC personnel ex=
amined, what representations they received, how they evaluated those repres=
entations, and whether staff identified lessons concerning existing cross-m=
arket safeguards. That information could materially change public understan=
ding of both the outage and the adequacy of the government's response.



=C2=A7 200.80(g)(12)(iii): There is no commercial interest in disclosure. W=
hyDRS is a nonprofit public-interest association. Neither WhyDRS nor I will=
 sell the records, place the resulting analysis behind a paywall, use the r=
ecords to provide a commercial data product, or obtain a financial advantag=
e from their release. The requested information is being sought for public =
research, publication, advocacy, and dissemination. The work is undertaken =
to educate the public about market structure, investor protection, operatio=
nal resilience, and systemic risk. The public interest is therefore the int=
erest primarily served by disclosure. There is no identified commercial int=
erest to weigh against it; in any event, the substantial public interest de=
scribed above would predominate.



This factor requires consideration of both the existence and the magnitude =
of any commercial interest. Here, there is no expected direct economic retu=
rn, customer relationship, proprietary use, exclusive access, or competitiv=
e advantage arising from disclosure. WhyDRS' interest is in what the record=
s reveal about government monitoring, coordination, and decision-making, no=
t in exploiting the records as a marketable commodity or using them to adva=
nce a private transaction.



The requested disclosure also is not connected to a revenue-producing deadl=
ine, contemplated transaction, client engagement, or private dispute. Its v=
alue to WhyDRS lies in improving the accuracy, evidentiary basis, and publi=
c usefulness of an existing editorial project. The same publication plan wo=
uld make the responsive information available regardless of whether it supp=
orts, complicates, or contradicts WhyDRS' present understanding of the outa=
ge.



That willingness to publish materially relevant information even when it cu=
ts against the requester's prior analysis further distinguishes this work f=
rom commercial exploitation. The objective is a better public account of th=
e government's response, not acquisition of selectively useful material to =
support a predetermined financial or competitive position.



Even if the Office identifies some indirect or incidental organizational be=
nefit from publication, that attenuated benefit would be secondary to the c=
oncrete public interest described above. Section 200.80(g)(12)(iii)(B) requ=
ires the Office to compare the identified commercial interest with the publ=
ic interest, and it ordinarily presumes that the public interest is primari=
ly served when a news-media requester satisfies the public-interest standar=
d. WhyDRS' planned open publication, prior editorial work, and completed pu=
blic program about this precise outage establish that public understanding=
=E2=80=94not economic return=E2=80=94is the interest primarily served by di=
sclosure.



For these reasons, disclosure is likely to contribute significantly to publ=
ic understanding of the SEC's operations and activities and is not primaril=
y in any commercial interest. I respectfully request a complete fee waiver.=
 If the Office concludes that only some categories satisfy the standard, I =
alternatively request a waiver or reduction for, at minimum, the records re=
flecting SEC monitoring, analysis, decision-making, and interagency or mark=
et-participant coordination.





Statutory limitation on search fees



I also request clarification of the Office's authority to assess any search=
 fees in connection with this request.



The Office received the request on December 3, 2025. The August 4, 2026 let=
ter identifies approximately 3,990 potentially responsive pages.



Under 5 USC =C2=A7 552(a)(4)(A)(viii) and =C2=A7 200.80(g)(4)(iv), search f=
ees may not be assessed after failure to comply with FOIA's applicable time=
 limits unless a statutory exception applies.



The Office's December 3, 2025 acknowledgment identifies three unusual circu=
mstances and states that the Office is invoking a ten-working-day extension=
. It does not, however, state the date on which a determination is expected=
 to be dispatched. Its statements that I would be notified "as soon as poss=
ible" and that the request would be processed in the order received do not =
supply an expected determination date.



=C2=A7=C2=A0552(a)(6)(B)(i) requires a written extension notice to set fort=
h both the unusual circumstances and "the date on which a determination is =
expected to be dispatched." Sections 552(a)(4)(A)(viii)(II)(aa) and (bb), i=
n turn, require timely written notice "in accordance with paragraph (6)(B).=
" The omission of an expected determination date therefore raises the thres=
hold question whether the December 3 acknowledgment qualifies as the notice=
 required by either fee exception. If the Office contends that the acknowle=
dgment satisfied =C2=A7 552(a)(6)(B)(i), please identify the expected deter=
mination date stated in that notice or explain the basis for treating the n=
otice as compliant despite its omission.



Even if the December 3 acknowledgment constituted compliant notice, subclau=
se (II)(aa) excuses noncompliance for only an additional ten days. After ex=
piration of that extended period, search fees may not be assessed unless an=
other exception in subclause (II) applies.



The record presently does not reflect that the Office has discussed with me=
 how I could effectively limit the scope of the request within the meaning =
of =C2=A7 552(a)(4)(A)(viii)(II)(bb). It reflects, at most, one Office-orig=
inated invitation to narrow=E2=80=94the August 4, 2026 letter=E2=80=94and d=
oes not reflect three good-faith attempts to initiate such a discussion.



Please state specifically whether the Office relies on subclause (II)(bb) o=
r subclause (II)(cc) to preserve search-fee authority. If the Office relies=
 on (bb), please identify the basis for contending that more than 5,000 pag=
es are necessary notwithstanding its approximately 3,990-page estimate, and=
 identify the dates and substance of every discussion or attempted contact =
on which it relies to satisfy the consultation or three-good-faith-attempt =
requirement. If the Office relies on (cc), please identify the court determ=
ination that exceptional circumstances exist and provide the court, case na=
me, docket number, date, and relevant order.



Pending resolution of these issues, please do not construe this corresponde=
nce as agreement to pay commercial-use search or review fees.



Please confirm that Request No. 26-00500-FOIA will remain open and be place=
d in the Complex Track while the fee-category and fee-waiver issues are res=
olved.







In good faith,



John Wooten

WhyDRS







P.S. Please produce responsive electronic records in their native or readil=
y reproducible electronic format. For email records, please provide .eml fi=
les preserving the complete available Internet message headers, MIME struct=
ure, message body, and relationship between each email and its attachments,=
 with attachments produced in their original native formats. Where .eml pro=
duction is not reasonably practicable because redactions are required, plea=
se provide searchable PDFs preserving the full available email header infor=
mation and the relationship between each email and its attachments.









